\NormalTok{                                                                                          1. Introduction}

\NormalTok{                                             Pluripotential theory is a branch of complex analysis in several variables that studies}
\NormalTok{                                          plurisubharmonic functions and their properties. It extends classical potential theory, which deals}
\NormalTok{                                          with harmonic and subharmonic functions in the setting of one complex variable. It has proven to}
\NormalTok{                                          be a strong tool in complex geometry with several applications, notably to the study of the complex}
\NormalTok{                                          Monge–Ampère equations in relation to the Calabi–Yau problem, but also in other areas of research}
\NormalTok{                                          of complex algebraic geometry (see for instance [Bou+10; BHJ17; BHJ19]), non{-}archimedean and}
\NormalTok{                                          Arakelov theory (see [BGK24; CLD25; YZ17]), and mirror symmetry [Li23].}
