\NormalTok{                                                       FIRST STEPS IN TROPICAL INTERSECTION THEORY}

\NormalTok{                                                                       LARS ALLERMANN AND JOHANNES RAU}
\NormalTok{arXiv:0709.3705v3 [math.AG] 20 Jan 2009}




\NormalTok{                                                   A BSTRACT. We establish first parts of a tropical intersection theory. Namely, we define}
\NormalTok{                                                   cycles, Cartier divisors and intersection products between these two (without passing to}
\NormalTok{                                                   rational equivalence) and discuss push{-}forward and pull{-}back. We do this first for fans}
\NormalTok{                                                   in Rn and then for “abstract” cycles that are fans locally. With regard to applications}
\NormalTok{                                                   in enumerative geometry, we finally have a look at rational equivalence and intersection}
\NormalTok{                                                   products of cycles and cycle classes in Rn .}




\NormalTok{                                                                                  1. I NTRODUCTION}

\NormalTok{                                          Tropical geometry is a recent development in the field of algebraic geometry that tries}
\NormalTok{                                          to transform algebro{-}geometric problems into easier, purely combinatorial ones. In the}
\NormalTok{                                          last few years various authors were able to answer questions of enumerative algebraic}
\NormalTok{                                          geometry using these techniques. In order to determine the number of (classical) curves}
\NormalTok{                                          meeting given conditions in some ambient space they constructed moduli spaces of tropical}
\NormalTok{                                          curves and had to intersect the corresponding tropical conditions in these moduli spaces.}
\NormalTok{                                          Since there is no tropical intersection theory yet the computation of the arising intersection}
\NormalTok{                                          multiplicities and the proof of the independence of the choice of the conditions had to be}
\NormalTok{                                          repeated for every single problem without the tools of an elaborated intersection theory}
\NormalTok{                                          (see for example [GM], [KM]).}
\NormalTok{                                          A first draft of a general tropical intersection theory without proofs has been presented}
\NormalTok{                                          by Mikhalkin in [M]. The concepts introduced there — if set up rigorously — would}
\NormalTok{                                          help to unify and solve the above mentioned problems and would provide utilities for}
\NormalTok{                                          further applications. Thus in this paper we develop in detail the basics of a general tropical}
\NormalTok{                                          intersection theory based on Mikhalkin’s ideas.}
\end{Highlighting}
\end{Shaded}

